            <ArticleTitle>Assessing Health Issues in States with Large Minority Populations.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>483-97</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cnur.2015.05.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0029-6465(15)00057-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Health care spending is often addressed in discussions of budgeting and deficits in the United States. It is important to many Americans that funds allocated for health care spending be allocated and spent in the most efficient and effective manner, leading to improved health outcomes, particularly for underserved populations. Many studies address health care spending, but few address the issue of spending as it relates to societal well-being, or certain health outcomes that adversely impact communities. This study seeks to expand the available literature by analyzing data from national sources at the state level.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
